  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="privacy">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Privacy and censorship</p>
      <h2>Internet privacy and censorship</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <h3>Privacy: who sees your data?</h3>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Privacy</p><p>Your right to control who collects, sees, and uses <b>information about you</b>: your name, location, photos, messages, and what you do online.</p></div>
      <p>Every time you go online, you leave data behind. Two common ways it is collected are <b>cookies</b> and your <b>digital footprint</b>:</p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Cookie</p><p>A small file that a website saves in your browser, so it can remember you: your login, language, or shopping cart.</p></div>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Digital footprint</p><p>The trail of data you leave online: posts, photos, comments, searches, and the data apps collect about you.</p></div>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus big"><Fragment set:html={cookiePic()} /><figcaption>First-party cookies are from the site you visit. Third-party cookies are from another company, usually for ads.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>In the Philippines, <b>RA 10173</b>, the <b>Data Privacy Act of 2012</b>, protects personal information. It says how organizations may collect and use it, gives people rights over their data (such as the right to be informed and to object), and created the <b>National Privacy Commission (NPC)</b> to enforce it.</p>
      <p>You can protect your own privacy with a few habits:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Check your <b>privacy settings</b> on social media: who can see your posts?</li>
        <li>Check <b>app permissions</b>: does a flashlight app really need your contacts and location?</li>
        <li>Choose <b>"reject"</b> or "necessary only" on cookie banners when you can.</li>
        <li><b>Think before you post:</b> what you upload can stay online for years and be seen by future employers.</li>
        <li>Know that <b>private (incognito) browsing</b> only stops your own device from saving your history. Websites and your ISP can still see your activity.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Censorship: who decides what you can see?</h3>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Internet censorship</p><p>Blocking, filtering, or removing online content so people cannot see or share it. It can be done by governments, by ISPs on official orders, or by platforms that remove posts.</p></div>
      <p>Countries censor in very different ways. Here are three real examples, from narrow (one kind of content) to broad (whole services):</p>
      <div class="fn-grid censor-grid">
        <div class="fn-card"><Fragment set:html={censorPic(1)} /><p class="fn-t">Protecting children</p><p>Under <b>RA 11930</b> (2022), Philippine ISPs must block child sexual abuse material within 24 hours of a proper notice and report it to the DOJ.</p></div>
        <div class="fn-card"><Fragment set:html={censorPic(2)} /><p class="fn-t">Blocking illegal businesses</p><p>In December 2025, at the request of the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, the NTC ordered ISPs to block 50 unregistered online crypto trading platforms.</p></div>
        <div class="fn-card"><Fragment set:html={censorPic(3)} /><p class="fn-t">Blocking whole services</p><p>China blocks many foreign sites and apps, including Google, Facebook, and YouTube.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="look">
        <p class="note-label">Two sides of the debate</p>
        <p>Supporters say some blocking protects people from crime, scams, and harmful content. Critics warn that blocking can go too far: it can silence news, criticism, or ideas the public has a right to see. Many people agree on blocking material like child abuse, but argue over where the line should be and <b>who</b> should draw it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Quick check: Part 7</h3>
        <p>Tap an answer to see why.</p>
        <Quiz id="quiz-p7" questions={part7} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="self-check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Self-check</p>
      <h2>Self-check: the Internet and the Web</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Use this to see what you remember from the whole lesson, then go back to any part you missed.</p>

      <h3>Do not confuse: summary</h3>
      <p>These pairs are often mixed up. The right column says what makes the two different:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>These two...</th><th>...are different because</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Internet vs. the Web</b></td><td>the worldwide network vs. one service on it: linked web pages</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>ARPANET vs. the Web</b></td><td>the 1969 network that grew into the Internet vs. the 1989 invention of web pages</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>IP address vs. domain name</b></td><td>a number for computers vs. a name for people; DNS links the two</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Domain name vs. URL</b></td><td>a whole website vs. one page on it</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>IPv4 vs. IPv6</b></td><td>32 bits, about 4.3 billion addresses vs. 128 bits, far more</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>ICANN vs. NTC</b></td><td>coordinates names and numbers worldwide vs. regulates Philippine telecom</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>GEO vs. LEO</b></td><td>about 35,786 km up, more delay vs. below 1,000 km, less delay</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Bandwidth vs. latency</b></td><td>how much data per second vs. how long the delay is</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Browser vs. search engine</b></td><td>the app that shows web pages vs. a website that finds them</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Reference vs. in-text citation</b></td><td>the full entry at the end vs. (Author, date) in your sentence</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Privacy vs. censorship</b></td><td>who sees YOUR data vs. what content you are ALLOWED to see</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Try it at home</h3>
      <p>No need to submit anything:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Run a speed test on your phone (search for "speed test"). Write down the download speed, upload speed, and ping. Try again at a different time of day.</li>
        <li>Search <span class="mono">site:gov.ph</span> plus a topic you care about. Compare the results with a normal search.</li>
        <li>Open your phone's settings and check which apps can use your location.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Final quiz</h3>
      <p>Twelve questions: eleven from this lesson and one that mixes both lessons.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-final" questions={final} />

      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Evolution:</b> ARPANET (1969) → TCP/IP (1983) → the Web (1989; free in 1993) → the Philippines online (1994). Today about 6 billion people are online, and no one owns the whole Internet.</li>
          <li><b>Community:</b> users, ISPs, infrastructure companies, content and cloud providers, hardware and software makers, and governments; ICANN, IETF, W3C, ISOC, and APNIC coordinate. In the Philippines, the DICT sets policy and the NTC regulates.</li>
          <li><b>Identification:</b> IP addresses (IPv4, IPv6) for devices; domain names and URLs for sites and pages, linked to IP addresses by DNS; usernames and email addresses for people.</li>
          <li><b>Connecting:</b> fiber, DSL, cable, mobile data, fixed wireless, and satellite (GEO or LEO); compare ISPs on availability, speed, latency, data limits, cost, contract, and support.</li>
          <li><b>Searching and citing:</b> specific keywords and operators (" ", -, OR, site:, filetype:); APA references: Author. (Date). <i>Title</i>. Site. URL.</li>
          <li><b>Activities:</b> messaging, VoIP and video calls, social media, forums, podcasts; e-learning, e-commerce, e-banking, e-government, and more.</li>
          <li><b>Privacy and censorship:</b> cookies and your digital footprint; RA 10173 and the NPC; blocking ranges from narrow (child protection) to broad (whole services).</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next:</b> <a href={base + course.href}>Back to the IT 110 lessons</a>. Network and Internet security comes after the midterm.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

